.TH CBMAP_INIT_FULL 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_init_full \- create an ordered map with a custom allocator and comparison into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_init_full(hm_name, mmgmt_procs, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates an ordered map that takes all of its memory from mmgmt_procs and
orders its keys with custom_comparison_proc. The macro then assigns that map
to hm_name. The caller must declare the handle first, with cbmap_declare(3)
or cbmap_declare_scoped(3). The key type and the value type do not come to
this macro as arguments. The macro reads them from the companion type
variables that the declare macro created.

This is the widest of the init macros. cbmap_init(3) is this macro with the
default allocator and the default order. cbmap_init_mp(3) gives only an
allocator, and cbmap_init_cc(3) gives only a comparison procedure. Each of
the two arguments can be NULL, which selects the matching default. This one
macro therefore covers every combination.

The one-step equivalents are cbmap_construct_full(3) and
cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 The map handle that the caller declared before.
                        The macro creates the map into it
mmgmt_procs             Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                        default allocator
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for the declared key type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to a statement and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key. It returns a positive value when
the first key orders after the second key. It returns zero when the map must
treat the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update and not an
insert. The procedure therefore defines key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over the key type. That order
must not change while the map holds elements. The procedure gets two bare
pointers and no lengths. It can only read a width that the declared key type
fixes. The map validates the width of an incoming key against that
type. The map also aligns its stored keys for that type.
cbmap_create_full(3) describes the default orders that a procedure replaces.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must not be NULL when the caller
gives one. The map copies the procedures into itself. The procedures and
the comparison procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that the
map got. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
Only the key type reaches the container. Only the type-inferred macros read the
declared value type
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

cbmap_declare(releases, version, char *);
cbmap_init_full(releases, &procs, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_init (3),
.BR cbmap_init_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_init_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_full (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
